---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import AboutShell from '../../components/AboutShell.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/ui';

const t = useTranslations('en');
const description =
  'Senior full-stack engineer. Capability architecture, production infrastructure, and AI developer tooling.';
---

<BaseLayout title={`${t('about.title')} — Cristopher Martinez`} description={description}>
  <SiteHeader />
  <AboutShell heading={t('about.title')} lede={description}>
    <h2>Loopay — since October 2022</h2>
    <p>
      Payments infrastructure at <a href="https://loopay.com/" rel="noopener">Loopay</a>. Money in, money out, and everything that has to hold when it does
      not go through the first time. I work across the stack — React on the front, a GraphQL and
      Prisma API behind it, AWS Lambda deployed through CDK, CI pipelines and end-to-end suites —
      inside an engineering group of roughly forty people, on a codebase past ten thousand commits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The problems that have taken most of my time are the unglamorous ones: bulk payment
      reversals, webhook delivery when the other side is free to retry, balance reconciliation,
      rate limiting, and audit trails that have to survive being questioned. Observability is
      OpenTelemetry end to end, database spans included.
    </p>
    <p>
      One detail I learned early: an amount is only as reliable as the place it enters. In my
      first months I made the payout builder refuse ambiguous amounts — a dot or a comma is
      accepted only as the decimal separator, and anything else is stopped at its row and column
      before a batch exists.
    </p>

    <h2>Systems, not features</h2>
    <p>
      I split a video platform into eight packages that talk to each other through published
      contracts: four core capabilities, plus contracts, configuration, observability and
      monetization. Products import only the public index of each capability, and none reaches
      into a capability's internals today. A lint rule that would forbid it lives in the shared
      preset; wiring it into every app's CI is the piece still missing. In velocityanime,
      dependencies are wired in a single composition root and passed down as ports.
    </p>

    <h2>I ship it and I run it</h2>
    <p>
      Two products answer on their own domains today, on infrastructure I operate: VPS, Docker,
      nginx, Prometheus and Grafana. Both grew out of one monorepo — eight packages,
      2,075 commits with merges excluded, 658 test files — and each now ships from its own
      repository, with its own CI and deploy pipeline.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also audited it against myself — thirteen modules, 112 findings, 20 of them critical,
      ordered by a dependency graph. That audit says more about how I work than clean
      code would: I go looking for my own mistakes before somebody else finds them.
    </p>

    <h2>AI as infrastructure, not as an API call</h2>
    <p>
      My AI work sits a layer below the chat endpoint: renting GPUs by the hour to run
      open-weight models my own machine cannot hold, with a reconciler that re-adopts a box still
      billing after a crash; a core package that clusters face embeddings per tenant and throws
      rather than silently merging people across tenants, not yet wired into the product; and a
      retrieval system that gives coding agents durable project memory over MCP.
    </p>
    <p>
      That last one is a full RAG pipeline rather than a vector-database wrapper: hybrid retrieval
      that blends cosine similarity with keyword scoring and then reranks, chunking sized by
      content type because code truncated mid-function retrieves as noise, and an embedding
      provider registry that falls back to a local TF-IDF implementation so the system still works
      with no API key and no network. Thirty-one tools over MCP, 1,304 tests.
    </p>

    <h2>Before that, and the rest of the sheet</h2>
    <p>
      Independent web design and development from 2019 to 2022 — the years where I learned that
      a client does not care how the site is built until it is slow on their phone, which is why
      this one is measured before it is shipped. Software engineering at Universidad Santa María.
      Spanish is my first language; I work in English daily.
    </p>
    <p>Caracas, Venezuela. Remote-friendly, and open to Latin America and beyond.</p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      Concepts before code. I would rather spend an afternoon understanding why something breaks
      than an hour patching around it, because the patch comes back and the understanding does not.
      I write the check that fails when the logic breaks, and I keep the shortcuts visible instead
      of pretending they are not there.
    </p>
  </AboutShell>
</BaseLayout>
